# DTF tier manifest: auth-logout-revokes-access-token  (artifact-keeper#4883; 1.12.0)
# Profile-set: filesystem / single. No extra services.
# Sibling of auth-logout-revokes-session (#4831), which covers the refresh
# token; this tier covers the access token of the same session.
#
# THE BEHAVIOUR
# POST /api/v1/auth/logout must end the session's ACCESS token too, not only
# its refresh-token family. Before the fix the access token is a stateless JWT
# and a Bearer replay of it keeps answering 200 for its whole lifetime
# (JWT_ACCESS_TOKEN_EXPIRY_MINUTES, 30 by default) after a 200 logout. The fix
# records the session's family at logout (and at refresh-token replay
# detection) and refuses access tokens of that family; other replicas follow
# within SESSION_REVOCATION_CACHE_TTL_SECS (default 5 s).
#
# THE GATES
#   (FIX)      after a 200 logout, a Bearer replay of the session's access
#              token on GET /api/v1/auth/me is 401 within 5 s (polled every
#              0.5 s), for the API shape (Bearer + `{}` body) and the browser
#              shape (the login's cookie jar, then the cookie's access token
#              replayed as a Bearer header). RED on 1.11.5: 200 for 5 s.
#   (FIX)      refresh-token replay detection (a consumed refresh token whose
#              successor was also used) ends the access tokens of that
#              family within 5 s. RED on 1.11.5: 200.
#   (CONTROL)  both images: the access token answers 200 before logout; a
#              second session of the same user still answers 200 after the
#              first one logs out (per-session scoping); a password change
#              ends the old access token (credential watermark, 401 within
#              5 s); an API token of the user is not affected by logout.
#
# RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED=false: about ten logins and refreshes back-to-back.
PROFILES="storage.filesystem"
ORACLE="oracle.sh"
RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED="false"
